\v 34 I'll bring them again into the¦47204 land¦47205 which¦47206 I promised to¦47207 their fathers¦47209, to Avraham, to Yitshak \add !(Isaac)\add*, and to Ya'akov \add !(Jacob)\add*, and they'll rule over it. I'll increase them, and they won't¦47227 be diminished.
\v 35 And¦47229 I'll make an eternal¦47233 covenant¦47232 with them to¦47235 be¦47235 their god¦47239, and¦47240 they'll be¦47242 my people¦47245. I'll no¦47247 more remove my people¦47251 Yisrael out of the¦47255 land¦47256 that¦47257 I've given¦47258 them.”
\c 3
\p
\v 1 Oh master Almighty¦47261, you God¦47263 of Yisrael, the soul¦47265 in¦47266 anguish and¦47268 the troubled spirit¦47269 cries¦47271 to¦47272 you¦47273.
\v 2 Hear, Oh master, and¦47276 have mercy; for you're a merciful God. Yes, have mercy on us, because¦47278 we've sinned before you¦47281.
\v 3 Because¦47282 you're¦47283 enthroned forever, and¦47287 we¦47288 keep perishing.
\v 4 Oh master Almighty¦47293, you God¦47295 of Yisrael, hear¦47312 now the¦47299 prayer¦47300 of the¦47301 dead Israelis, and¦47304 of the¦47306 children¦47305 of¦47308 those who were sinners before you¦47309, who¦47310 didn't listen to the¦47313 voice¦47314 of you¦47315 their god¦47316; because of this, these \add ≈deadly diseases\add* cling to us.
\v 5 Don't¦47323 remember¦47324 the iniquities of our fathers¦47326, but¦47328 remember¦47329 your power and¦47332 your name¦47333 at this¦47336 time¦47337.
\v 6 Because¦47339 you're¦47340 the¦47342 master our god¦47343, and¦47345 we'll¦47344 praise you¦47347, Oh master.
\v 7 Because¦47350 this cause, you've put your fear¦47354 in our hearts, to¦47356 the intent that we¦47358 should call¦47360 on¦47360 your name¦47362. We'll praise you in¦47367 our captivity, for we've¦47370 called to mind¦47374 all¦47376 the¦47380 iniquity of¦47382 our fathers¦47378 who sinned before you¦47383.
\v 8 Listen, we're¦47385 yet this day in¦47387 our captivity where you've scattered us, for a reproach¦47396 and¦47397 a curse, and¦47400 to be subject to penalty according¦47403 to¦47403 all¦47404 the¦47405 iniquities of our fathers¦47407 who¦47409 departed from¦47411 the master our god¦47413.
\s1 Finding the way to wisdom
\p
\v 9 Hear¦47415, Oh Yisrael, the commandments¦47417 of life¦47418! Give ear to understand¦47420 wisdom!
\v 10 How is it, Oh Yisrael, that you're in your enemies’ land, that you've become old in a strange country, that you're defiled with the dead,
\v 11 that you're counted with those who are in Haidais?
\v 12 You've forsaken the fountain of wisdom¦47448.
\v 13 If you'd walked in the¦47449 way of¦47451 God¦47452, you'd have dwelled in¦47457 peace¦47458 forever.
\v 14 Learn where¦47462 there's¦47463 wisdom¦47464, where¦47465 there's¦47466 strength¦47467, and where¦47468 there's¦47469 understanding¦47470, that you may also know¦47472 where¦47474 there's¦47475 length of days and¦47477 life¦47478, where¦47479 there's¦47480 the light¦47481 of the eyes¦47482 and¦47483 peace¦47484.
\v 15 Who's found¦47486 out her place¦47488? Who's¦47494 come into her treasuries¦47495?
\v 16 Where¦47497 are¦47498 the¦47499 princes of the¦47501 heathen, and¦47503 those who¦47504 ruled the¦47506 \add wild\add* animals¦47507 that¦47508 are on¦47509 the¦47510 earth¦47511,
\v 17 those who¦47512 had their pastime with the¦47514 fowls of¦47516 the¦47516 air, and¦47519 those who¦47520 hoarded up silver and¦47523 gold¦47525, in which¦47526 people trust, and¦47529 of their getting there's no¦47530 end?
\v 18 Because¦47536 those who diligently sought silver, and¦47541 were so anxious, and¦47543 whose works¦47548 are¦47545 past finding¦47546 out¦47546,
\v 19 they've vanished and¦47551 gone down¦47554 to Haidais, and others have come¦47554 up in their place.
\p
\v 20 Younger men have seen the light¦47562 and¦47563 lived on¦47565 the¦47566 earth¦47567, but¦47569 they haven't known¦47572 the way of knowledge,
\v 21 nor understood¦47574 its paths¦47575. Their children¦47581 haven't embraced it. They're far¦47587 off from their way.
\v 22 It hasn't¦47589 been heard¦47590 of in¦47591 Kena'an \add !(Canaan)\add*, neither has it been seen¦47594 in¦47595 Teyman.
\v 23 The¦47600 sons¦47598 also of Hagar who¦47602 seek understanding¦47603, which are in the land¦47607, the¦47608 merchants¦47609 of Merran and¦47612 Teyman, and¦47614 the¦47615 authors of fables, and¦47617 the searchers out of understanding¦47621—none of these have known¦47626 the way of wisdom¦47624 or remembered¦47628 her paths¦47630.
\p
\v 24 Oh¦47632 Yisrael \add !(Israel)\add*, how¦47634 great¦47635 is God's¦47639 house¦47637! How large is the¦47642 place¦47643 of his possession!
\v 25 It's great¦47647 and¦47648 has no¦47649 end. It's high¦47652 and¦47653 unmeasurable.
\v 26 Giants were born that were¦47663 famous of old, great of stature, and expert in war¦47666.
\v 27 God¦47671 didn't¦47672 choose these, nor did he¦47668 give¦47675 the¦47670 way of knowledge to¦47675 them,
\v 28 so¦47677 they perished, because they had¦47682 no wisdom. They perished through¦47685 their own foolishness.
\v 29 Who's¦47692 gone up into heaven¦47693, taken¦47695 her, and¦47697 brought her down from¦47700 the¦47701 clouds¦47702?
\v 30 Who's gone over the¦47706 sea¦47707, found¦47709 her, and¦47711 will bring¦47712 her for choice gold¦47714?
\v 31 There's no¦47716 one who¦47718 knows¦47719 her way, nor any who¦47720 comprehend her path¦47721.
\v 32 But¦47729 he that knows all¦47733 things knows¦47734 her, he found her out with his understanding¦47739. He¦47740 who¦47741 prepared¦47742 the¦47743 earth¦47744 for all time¦47748 has filled¦47749 it¦47750 with four-footed¦47752 animals¦47751.
\v 33 It's he who¦47753 sends forth the¦47755 light¦47756, and¦47757 it goes. He called¦47759 it¦47760, and¦47761 it¦47763 obeyed¦47762 him with fear.
\v 34 The¦47765 stars¦47767 shone in¦47769 their watches¦47771, and¦47773 were glad. When he¦47776 called¦47775 them, they said¦47778, “Here we are.” They shone with¦47781 gladness¦47782 to him who made¦47784 them.
\v 35 This is our god¦47788. No¦47790 other¦47792 can be compared to¦47793 him.
\v 36 He's found out all¦47796 the way of knowledge, and¦47799 has given¦47800 it to Ya'akov \add !(Jacob)\add* his servant and¦47806 to Yisrael \add !(Israel)\add* who's loved¦47809 by¦47810 him.
\v 37 Afterward she¦47813 appeared on¦47814 earth¦47816, and¦47818 lived with men.
\c 4
\p
\v 1 This¦47823 is the¦47824 book¦47825 of¦47828 God's¦47829 commandments and¦47830 the¦47831 law¦47832 that endures forever. All¦47838 those who¦47839 hold it¦47841 fast will live¦47843, but those who leave¦47846 it'll¦47847 die.
\v 2 Turn, Oh Ya'akov, and¦47851 take hold of it¦47853. Walk towards the¦47856 shining of¦47859 its light¦47860.
\v 3 Don't¦47862 give¦47863 your glory¦47866 to another¦47873, nor the¦47869 things that are to your advantage to a foreign nation¦47872.
\v 4 Oh Yisrael, we're¦47875 happy¦47874; for the things that¦47877 are pleasing¦47879 to God¦47881 are¦47884 made known to us.
\s1 Yerushalem mourns for her children
\p
\v 5 Be of good cheer, my people¦47886, the memorial¦47888 of Yisrael.
\v 6 You weren't sold to the¦47891 nations¦47892 for destruction¦47895, but because¦47896 you¦47899 moved God¦47901 to¦47902 anger, you were delivered¦47902 to your adversaries.
\v 7 Because¦47906 you provoked him who¦47907 made you¦47909 by sacrificing to demons¦47911 and¦47912 not to God¦47914.
\v 8 You forgot the eternal¦47920 God¦47919 who brought you up. You also grieved Yerushalem \add !(Jerusalem)\add*, who¦47924 nursed you.
\v 9 Because she saw the¦47930 anger¦47933 that came on you from¦47934 God¦47936, and¦47937 said¦47938, “Listen¦47939, you who live near Tsiyon \add !(Zion)\add*; for God¦47946 has brought on me great¦47948 mourning.
\v 10 Because¦47950 I've seen the¦47951 captivity¦47952 of my sons¦47954 and¦47956 daughters¦47958, which¦47959 the¦47962 Eternal¦47963 has brought on them.
\v 11 Because¦47965 with¦47967 joy¦47968 I nourished them, but¦47970 sent them away with¦47971 weeping and¦47973 mourning.
\v 12 Let no man rejoice over me, a widow¦47979 and¦47980 forsaken by¦47982 many. Because¦47985 the¦47986 sins¦47987 of my children¦47989, I'm¦47990 left desolate, because¦47991 they turned¦47992 away from God's¦47995 law¦47994
\v 13 and¦47996 had no¦47999 regard for his statutes. They didn't¦48001 walk in the ways of God's¦48005 commandments¦48004 or tread in the paths¦48007 of discipline¦48008 in¦48009 his righteousness¦48010.
\v 14 Let those who¦48014 live near Tsiyon come and¦48017 remember¦48018 the¦48019 captivity¦48020 of my sons¦48022 and¦48024 daughters¦48025, which¦48026 the¦48029 Eternal¦48030 has brought on them.
\v 15 Because¦48032 he's¦48034 brought a nation¦48035 on¦48033 them from¦48036 afar¦48036, a shameless nation¦48037 with a strange language, who didn't¦48045 respect old men or pity children¦48046.
\v 16 They've carried away¦48049 the¦48050 dear beloved sons of the¦48052 widow¦48053, and¦48054 left her who was alone¦48059 desolate of her daughters¦48057.”
\p
\v 17 But I—how can I help you?
\v 18 Because he who brought these calamities on you'll deliver you from your enemies' hand.
\v 19 Go your way, Oh my children¦48079. Go your way, for¦48082 I'm¦48081 left desolate.
\v 20 I've put off the garment of peace¦48089, and¦48091 put on the¦48093 sackcloth of my petition¦48094. I'll¦48095 cry to¦48097 the¦48098 Eternal¦48099 as long as I¦48103 live.
\s1 God will bring the exiles back
\p
\v 21 Take courage, my children¦48105. Cry to¦48107 God¦48109, and¦48110 he'll deliver you¦48112 from the power and hand¦48116 of the enemies¦48117.
\v 22 Because I've¦48118 trusted in the¦48122 Eternal, that¦48124 he'll save you¦48126; and¦48127 joy¦48130 has come to me from¦48131 the¦48132 Holy¦48133 One, because of the¦48135 mercy that'll¦48137 soon come to you¦48139 from¦48142 your Eternal¦48144 Saviour¦48145.
\v 23 Because¦48148 I¦48156 sent you¦48149 out with¦48150 mourning and¦48152 weeping, but¦48155 God¦48158 will give¦48154 you¦48159 to me again with¦48160 joy¦48163 and¦48162 gladness forever.
\v 24 Because as¦48167 now¦48169 those who live near Tsiyon \add !(Zion)\add* have seen your¦48175 captivity¦48176, so they'll shortly see¦48178 your salvation¦48186 from¦48182 our god¦48184 which¦48187 will come on you¦48189 with¦48190 great¦48192 glory¦48191 and¦48193 brightness of¦48195 the¦48195 Eternal¦48196.
\v 25 My children¦48197, suffer patiently¦48198 the¦48199 anger¦48205 that's come on you from¦48200 God¦48202, for your enemy¦48209 has persecuted you¦48204; but shortly you'll¦48207 see¦48211 his destruction¦48214 and¦48217 will tread on¦48218 their necks.
\v 26 My delicate ones have travelled¦48225 rough roads¦48226. They were taken away like a flock¦48230 carried off by¦48232 enemies¦48233.
\p
\v 27 Take courage, my children¦48235, and¦48236 cry to¦48238 God¦48240; for¦48242 you'll¦48243 be¦48241 remembered¦48247 by¦48244 him who's brought this on you.
\v 28 Because as¦48248 it was¦48250 your decision to¦48250 go astray from¦48257 God¦48259, return and seek¦48262 him ten times more.
\v 29 Because he who¦48264 brought these calamities on you'll¦48267 bring you¦48271 eternal¦48273 joy again with¦48275 your salvation¦48277.
\v 30 Take¦48279 courage¦48279, Oh Yerushalem, for he who¦48283 called you by name will comfort you.
\v 31 Miserable are those who¦48287 afflicted you¦48288 and¦48290 rejoiced at your¦48293 fall.
\v 32 Miserable are the¦48296 cities¦48297 which¦48298 your children¦48301 served. Miserable is she who¦48304 received¦48305 your sons¦48307.
\v 33 Because as¦48309 she rejoiced¦48311 at your¦48314 fall and¦48316 was glad of your¦48326 ruin, so she'll be grieved at her own desolation.
\v 34 And¦48328 I'll take away her pride in her great multitude and¦48335 her boasting will be turned into mourning.
\v 35 Because fire¦48341 will come on her from¦48345 the¦48346 Eternal¦48347 for many days¦48349; and¦48351 she'll be inhabited by¦48353 demons¦48354 for a long time¦48357.
\s1 Yerushalem's sorrow will turn to joy
\p
\v 36 Oh Yerushalem \add !(Jerusalem)\add*, look around you towards the east, and¦48362 see¦48363 the¦48366 joy that comes¦48371 to you from¦48367 God¦48369.
\v 37 Listen, your sons¦48375 come¦48373, whom¦48374 you¦48376 sent away¦48381. They come gathered together from¦48381 the east¦48382 to the west¦48384 at the¦48385 word of¦48387 the¦48387 Holy¦48388 One, rejoicing¦48389 in God's¦48392 glory¦48393.
\c 5
\p
\v 1 Take off the¦48396 garment of¦48398 your mourning and¦48400 affliction, Oh Yerushalem, and¦48404 put on forever the beauty of¦48410 the¦48410 glory¦48412 from God¦48411.
\v 2 Put on the robe of¦48421 the¦48421 righteousness¦48423 from God¦48422. Set on¦48427 your head¦48429 a diadem of the¦48431 glory¦48432 of¦48433 the¦48433 Eternal¦48434.
\v 3 Because¦48436 God¦48437 will show¦48438 your splendour everywhere¦48442 under¦48440 heaven¦48441.
\v 4 Because¦48447 your name¦48450 will be called¦48446 by God¦48453 forever “Righteous¦48458 Peace¦48457, Godly Glory¦48460”.
\p
\v 5 Arise¦48462, Oh Yerushalem, and¦48464 stand¦48465 on¦48466 the¦48467 height. Look¦48474 around you¦48478 towards the¦48476 east¦48483 and see your children¦48477 gathered from¦48479 the¦48484 going down of¦48486 the¦48486 sun¦48480 to its rising at the¦48489 word of¦48490 the¦48490 Holy¦48487 One, rejoicing that God¦48491 has remembered¦48492 them.
\v 6 Because they went¦48493 from¦48495 you¦48496 on foot, being led away by¦48499 their enemies¦48500, but¦48502 God¦48505 brings them in to¦48506 you¦48507 carried on high with¦48509 glory¦48510, on a royal throne¦48512.
\v 7 Because¦48515 God¦48517 has appointed that every¦48519 high¦48521 mountain¦48520 and¦48522 the eternal hills should be made low, and¦48525 the valleys filled up to make the ground¦48531 level, that Yisrael \add !(Israel)\add* may go safely in¦48532 God's¦48538 glory¦48539.
\v 8 Moreover the¦48543 woods¦48547 and¦48545 every¦48546 sweet smelling tree have shaded Yisrael by God's¦48553 commandment.
\v 9 Because¦48555 God¦48557 will lead Yisrael with¦48559 joy¦48560 in the¦48561 light¦48562 of his glory¦48564 with¦48566 the mercy and¦48568 righteousness¦48569 that¦48570 come from¦48571 him.
\c 6
\s1 The Letter of Yirmiyah (Yirmeyah/Jeremiah)
\p
\v 1 A copy of a letter that Yirmiyah \add !(Jeremiah)\add* sent¦48576 to¦48578 those who¦48579 were to be led captives into Bavel \add !(Babylon)\add* by¦48584 the¦48585 king¦48586 of the¦48587 Babylonians, to¦48589 give them the message¦48589 that God¦48604 commanded him.
\p
\v 2 Because of the sins which you've committed before God, you'll be¦48618 led away captives to Bavel by Nevukadnetsar \add !(Nebuchadnezzar)\add* king of the Babylonians.
\v 3 So when you come to¦48638 Bavel, you'll remain there many years, and for a long season, even for seven generations. After that¦48652, I'll bring you out peacefully from there.
\v 4 But now you'll see in Bavel gods of silver, gold, wood carried on shoulders, which cause the nations to fear.
\v 5 Beware therefore that you in no way become like these foreigners. Don't let fear take hold of you because of them when you see the multitude before them and behind them, worshipping them.
\v 6 But¦48693 say in your hearts, “O master, we¦48688 must worship you.”
\v 7 Because my angel is with you, and I myself care for¦48699 your souls.
\s1 Lifeless idols dressed like kings
\p
\v 8 Because their tongue is polished by the workman, and¦48717 they themselves are overlaid with gold¦48722 and with silver; yet they're only fake, and can't speak.
\v 9 And taking gold, as if it were for a virgin who loves to be happy, they make crowns for the heads of their gods.
\v 10 Sometimes also the priests take gold and silver from their gods, and spend it on themselves.
\v 11 They'll even give some of it to the common prostitutes. They dress them like men with clothes, even the gods of silver, gods of gold, and gods of wood.
\v 12 Yet these gods can't save themselves from rust and¦48799 moths, even though they're covered with¦48812 purple clothes.
\v 13 They wipe their faces because of the dust of the temple, which is thick on them.
\v 14 And he who can't¦48833 put to¦48835 death one who offends against him holds a sceptre, as though he¦48836 were judge of a country.
\v 15 He has also a dagger in his right hand, and an axe, but can't deliver himself from war and robbers.
\v 16 By this they're known not to be gods. Therefore don't fear them.
\v 17 Because like¦48866 a vessel that¦48890 a man¦48867 uses is¦48871 worth nothing when it's¦48880 broken, even so it's with their gods. When they're set up in the temples¦48879, their eyes are full of dust through¦48891 the feet of those who come in.
\v 18 As the courtyards are secured on every side on him who offends the king, as being committed to¦48897 suffer death, even so the priests secure their temples with doors, with locks, and¦48898 bars, so that¦48902 robbers won't carry them off.
\v 19 They light candles for them, yes, more than for themselves, even though they can't see one¦48907.
\v 20 They're like one of¦48939 the¦48939 beams of¦48941 the¦48941 temple. Men say their hearts are eaten out when things creeping out of the¦48943 earth devour both them and their clothing. They don't feel it¦48937
\v 21 when¦48949 their faces are blackened through the¦48951 smoke that¦48958 comes out of the¦48963 temple.
\v 22 Bats, swallows, and birds land on their bodies and heads. So¦48972 do the cats.
\v 23 By this you may know that they're no¦48988 gods. Therefore don't fear them.
\v 24 Notwithstanding the gold with which¦49002 they're covered to make them beautiful, unless someone wipes off the tarnish, they won't shine; for they didn't even feel it when they were¦49004 molten.
\v 25 Things in which there's no breath are bought at any cost.
\v 26 Having no feet, they're carried on shoulders. By this, they declare to men that they're worth nothing.
\v 27 Those who serve them are also ashamed, for if they fall to the ground at any time, they can't rise up again by themselves. If they're bowed down, they can't make themselves straight; but the offerings are set before them, as if they were dead men.
\v 28 And the things that are sacrificed to them, their priests sell and spend. In the same way, their wives also lay up part of it in salt; but to the poor and to the impotent they give none of it.
\v 29 The menstruous woman¦49102 and the woman in childbed touch their sacrifices, knowing therefore by these things that¦49101 they're no gods¦49104. Don't fear them.
\v 30 Because how can they be called gods? Because women set food before the gods of silver, gold, and wood.
\v 31 And¦49135 in¦49137 their temples the¦49138 priests sit on seats, having their clothes torn and their heads and beards shaven, and nothing on their heads.
\v 32 They roar and¦49157 cry before their gods, as men do at the¦49158 feast when one is dead.
\v 33 The priests also take off clothes from them and clothe their wives and children with them.
\v 34 Whether it's evil or good what one does to them, they aren't¦49183 able¦49184 to¦49185 repay it¦49189. They can't set up a¦49188 king or put him down.
\v 35 In the same way, they can neither¦49202 give riches nor money. Though a¦49198 man¦49198 make a vow to them and doesn't keep it, they'll never exact it.
\v 36 They can save no¦49212 man¦49217 from death. They can't¦49220 deliver the weak from the mighty.
\v 37 They can't¦49224 restore a blind man to¦49229 his sight, or deliver anyone who's in distress.
\v 38 They can show no mercy to the widow, or do good to the fatherless.
\v 39 They're like the stones that are cut out of the mountain, these gods of wood that are overlaid with gold and with silver. Those who minister to them will be confounded.
\s1 Why idols aren't gods
\p
\v 40 How could a man then think or say that they're gods, when even the Kasdim \add !(Chaldeans)\add* themselves dishonour them?
\v 41 If they see one mute who can't speak, they bring him and¦49283 ask him to call on Bel, as though he were¦49285 able¦49285 to¦49293 understand.
\v 42 Yet they can't perceive this themselves, and forsake them; for they have no understanding.
\v 43 The women also with cords around them sit in the ways, burning bran for incense; but if any of them, drawn by someone who passes by, lies with him, she reproaches her fellow, that she wasn't thought as worthy as herself and her cord wasn't broken.
\v 44 Whatever is¦49332 done among¦49333 them is¦49335 false. How¦49337 could a man then¦49338 think or¦49340 say that¦49342 they're gods¦49343?
\v 45 They're fashioned by¦49346 carpenters and¦49348 goldsmiths. They can be nothing else than¦49356 what the workmen make them to¦49362 be¦49362.
\v 46 And¦49364 they themselves who¦49365 fashioned them can never continue long. How¦49372 then¦49373 should the¦49376 things that are fashioned by¦49377 them?
\v 47 Because they've left¦49380 lies and¦49383 reproaches to those who¦49385 come after.
\v 48 Because¦49388 when¦49393 there comes any war¦49392 or \add ≈deadly disease\add* onto them, the¦49398 priests¦49399 consult with themselves, where¦49400 they may be hidden with¦49402 them.
\v 49 How¦49404 then¦49405 can't men understand that¦49409 they're no¦49410 gods¦49412, which¦49413 can't save themselves from¦49417 war or from¦49420 \add ≈deadly diseases\add*?
\v 50 Because seeing they're¦49422 only wood¦49424 and overlaid with¦49430 gold and silver, it'll¦49431 be known¦49429 in the future that¦49432 they're false.
\v 51 It'll be¦49433 manifest¦49441 to¦49442 all¦49437 nations¦49436 and¦49439 kings¦49440 that¦49443 they're no¦49444 gods¦49446, but¦49447 the works¦49448 of men's hands¦49449, and¦49451 that there's no work of God¦49453 in¦49455 them.
\v 52 Who then¦49459 may not know that¦49462 they aren't¦49464 gods¦49465?
\p
\v 53 Because¦49467 they can't¦49470 set up a king¦49466 in a¦49474 land or give¦49477 rain¦49473 to¦49477 men.
\v 54 They can't judge¦49478 their own cause, or redress a wrong, being¦49489 unable; for¦49491 they're like crows between heaven¦49495 and¦49496 earth¦49498.
\v 55 Because¦49500 even when¦49501 fire¦49511 falls¦49502 on the¦49507 house¦49504 of gods of wood overlaid with gold or¦49509 with silver, their priests will flee¦49515 away, and¦49516 escape, but¦49519 they themselves will be burnt apart like¦49520 beams.
\v 56 Moreover they can't withstand any king or enemies. How could a man then admit or think that they're gods?
\v 57 Those gods of wood overlaid with silver or with gold aren't able to escape from thieves or robbers.
\v 58 The¦49558 gold¦49559, silver, and clothes with which¦49554 they're clothed¦49565—those who¦49555 are¦49556 strong¦49556 will take from them, and go¦49569 away¦49569 with them. They won't be able to help themselves.
\v 59 Therefore it's better to¦49578 be¦49578 a king¦49579 who¦49581 shows his manhood, or¦49584 else a vessel¦49585 in¦49586 a house¦49587 profitable¦49588 for whatever the¦49592 owner needs, than¦49594 such false¦49596 gods¦49597—or¦49598 even a door¦49600 in¦49601 a house¦49602, to keep the¦49604 things safe that are in¦49605 it¦49606, than¦49608 such false¦49610 gods¦49611; or better to be a pillar¦49614 of wood in¦49615 a palace¦49616 than¦49617 such false¦49619 gods¦49620.
\p
\v 60 Because sun¦49621, moon¦49625, and¦49626 stars¦49627, being¦49628 bright¦49629 and¦49630 sent¦49631 to do their jobs, are¦49635 obedient.
\v 61 Likewise¦49636 also¦49637 the lightning¦49638 when¦49639 it¦49645 flashes is¦49642 beautiful to¦49651 see. In the same way, the wind also blows¦49651 in¦49648 every¦49649 country¦49650.
\v 62 And when God commands the clouds to go over the whole world, they do as they're told.
\v 63 And the fire sent from above to consume mountains and woods does as it's commanded; but these are to be compared to them neither in show nor power.
\v 64 Therefore a¦49709 man¦49709 shouldn't think or say that they're¦49696 gods¦49698, seeing they aren't able to¦49705 judge¦49705 causes or to¦49708 do¦49708 good to men.
\v 65 Knowing¦49710 therefore¦49711 that¦49712 they're no¦49713 gods¦49715, don't¦49716 fear¦49717 them.
\v 66 Because they can neither¦49719 curse nor bless kings¦49721.
\v 67 They can't show signs¦49728 in the heavens¦49733 among the nations¦49731, or shine as the¦49739 sun¦49740, or give light as the¦49745 moon¦49746.
\v 68 The¦49747 wild¦49748 animals¦49748 are better than they; for they can get under a covert, and help themselves.
\v 69 In no way¦49762 then is¦49764 it manifest¦49765 to us that¦49766 they're¦49767 gods¦49768. Therefore don't¦49770 fear¦49771 them.
\v 70 Because¦49774 as a scarecrow in¦49775 a garden of cucumbers that¦49781 keeps nothing, so are their gods¦49782 of wood overlaid with gold and silver.
\v 71 Likewise¦49803 also their gods¦49812 of wood overlaid with gold and¦49793 with silver, are like a white thorn in¦49795 an orchard that¦49799 every¦49800 bird¦49801 sits on¦49798. They're also like a dead body that's thrown out into¦49808 the¦49811 dark¦49809.
\v 72 You'll know them to be no¦49832 gods¦49834 by the¦49821 bright purple¦49822 that¦49831 rots on¦49827 them. They themselves will be¦49833 consumed afterwards, and will be¦49841 a reproach in¦49843 the¦49844 country¦49845.
\v 73 Better therefore is the just¦49849 man¦49848 who has no¦49850 idols¦49852; for¦49854 he'll be far from¦49856 reproach.
